cy code, in Linux 2.0 and 2.2\\nit was possible to set the\\n.B SO_BSDCOMPAT\\n.B SOL_SOCKET\\noption to receive remote errors only when the socket has been\\nconnected (except for\\n.B EPROTO\\nand\\n.BR EMSGSIZE ).\\nLocally generated errors are always passed.\\nSupport for this socket option was removed in later kernels; see\\n.BR socket (7)\\nfor further information.\\n.P\\nWhen the\\n.B IP_RECVERR\\noption is enabled, all errors are stored in the socket error queue,\\nand can be received by\\n.BR recvmsg (2)\\nwith the\\n.B MSG_ERRQUEUE\\nflag set.\\n.SS /proc interfaces\\nSystem-wide UDP parameter settings can be accessed by files in the directory\\n.IR /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ .\\n.TP\\n.IR udp_mem \\\" (since Linux 2.6.25)\\\"\\nThis is a vector of three integers governing the number\\nof pages allowed for queueing by all UDP sockets.\\n.RS\\n.TP\\n.I min\\nBelow this number of pages, UDP is not bothered about its\\nmemory appetite.\\nWhen the amount of memory allocated by UDP exceeds\\nthis number, UDP starts to moderate memory usage.\\n.TP\\n.I pressure\\nThis value was introduced to follow the format of\\n.I tcp_mem\\n(see\\n.BR tcp (7)).\\n.TP\\n.I max\\nNumber of pages allowed for queueing by all UDP sockets.\\n.RE\\n.IP\\nDefaults values for these three items are\\ncalculated at boot time from the amount of available memory.\\n.TP\\n.IR udp_rmem_min \\\" (integer; default value: PAGE_SIZE; since Linux 2.6.25)\\\"\\nMinimal size, in bytes, of receive buffers used by UDP sockets in moderation.\\nEach UDP socket is able to use the size for receiving data,\\neven if total pages of UDP sockets exceed\\n.I udp_mem\\npressure.\\n.TP\\n.IR udp_wmem_min \\\" (integer; default value: PAGE_SIZE; since Linux 2.6.25)\\\"\\nMinimal size, in bytes, of send buffer used by UDP sockets in moderation.\\nEach UDP socket is able to use the size for sending data,\\neven if total pages of UDP sockets exceed\\nmessage to arrive, unless the socket is nonblocking (see\\n.BR fcntl (2)),\\nin which case the value \\\\-1 is returned and\\n.I errno\\nis set to\\n.BR EAGAIN \\\" or \\\" EWOULDBLOCK .\\nThe receive calls normally return any data available, up to the requested\\namount, rather than waiting for receipt of the full amount requested.\\n.P\\nAn application can use\\n.BR select (2),\\n.BR poll (2),\\nor\\n.BR epoll (7)\\nto determine when more data arrives on a socket.\\n.SS The flags argument\\n--\\n.BR open (2).\\n.TP\\n.BR MSG_DONTWAIT \\\" (since Linux 2.2)\\\"\\nEnables nonblocking operation; if the operation would block,\\nthe call fails with the error\\n.BR EAGAIN \\\" or \\\" EWOULDBLOCK .\\nThis provides similar behavior to setting the\\n.B O_NONBLOCK\\nflag (via the\\n.BR fcntl (2)\\n.B F_SETFL\\noperation), but differs in that\\n.B MSG_DONTWAIT\\nis a per-call option, whereas\\n.B O_NONBLOCK\\nis a setting on the open file description (see\\n--\\nThese are some standard errors generated by the socket layer.\\nAdditional errors\\nmay be generated and returned from the underlying protocol modules;\\nsee their manual pages.\\n.TP\\n.BR EAGAIN \\\" or \\\" EWOULDBLOCK\\n.\\\\\\\" Actually EAGAIN on Linux\\nThe socket is marked nonblocking and the receive operation\\nwould block, or a receive timeout had been set and the timeout expired\\nbefore data was received.\\nPOSIX.1 allows either error to be returned for this case,\\nand does not require these constants to have the same value,\\nso a portable application should check for both possibilities.\\n.TP\\n.B EBADF\\nThe argument\\n.I sockfd\\nis an invalid file descriptor.\\n.TP\\n.B ECONNREFUSED\\nA remote host refused to allow the network connection (typically\\nbecause it is not running the requested service).\\n.TP\\n.B EFAULT\\nThe receive buffer pointer(s) point outside the process's\\naddress space.\\n.TP\\n.B EINTR\\nThe receive was interrupted by delivery of a signal before\\nany data was available; see\\n--\\n.TP\\n.B EINVAL\\nInvalid argument passed.\\n.\\\\\\\" e.g., msg_namelen < 0 for recvmsg() or addrlen < 0 for recvfrom()\\n.TP\\n.B ENOMEM\\nCould not allocate memory for\\n.BR recvmsg ().\\n.TP\\n.B ENOTCONN\\nThe socket is associated with a connection-oriented protocol\\nand has not been connected (see\\n.BR connect (2)\\nand\\n.BR accept (2)).\\n.TP\\n  [family]\\n  (if (= family af-inet6) 28 16))\\n\\n(defn make-sockaddr\\n  \\\"A freshly allocated sockaddr for the numeric address ip (a v4 or v6 literal,\\n  no scope zone) and port, as [sa len], or nil when ip is not a literal of\\n  family. scope-id, for a v6 address, is its sin6_scope_id. The caller frees\\n  sa.\\\"\\n  ([family ip port] (make-sockaddr family ip port 0))\\n  ([family ip port scope-id]\\n   (let [len (sockaddr-length family)\\n         sa  (ffi/alloc len)\\n         src (ffi/string->ptr (str ip))]\\n     (try\\n       (write-header! sa family len)\\n       (set-sockaddr-port! sa port)\\n       (if (= 1 (c-inet-pton family src (+ sa (if (= family af-inet6) 8 4))))\\n         (do (when (and (= family af-inet6) scope-id)\\n               (ffi/write sa :uint scope-id 24))\\n             [sa len])\\n         (do (ffi/free sa) nil))\\n       (finally (ffi/free src))))))\\n\\n(defn alloc-sockaddr\\n  \\\"A zeroed sockaddr_storage and its in/out length cell, as [sa lenp], ready for\\n  accept, getsockname or getpeername. Reset the cell to sockaddr-storage-size\\n  before reusing it. The caller frees both.\\\"\\n  []\\n  (let [sa (ffi/alloc sockaddr-storage-size)]\\n    [sa (int-cell sockaddr-storage-size)]))\\n\\n(defn- named-address [f fd]\\n  (let [[sa lenp] (alloc-sockaddr)]\\n    (try\\n      (let [[r _] (f fd sa lenp)]\\n        (when-not (neg? r)\\n          {:family (sockaddr-family sa) :ip (sockaddr-ip sa) :port (sockaddr-port sa)\\n           :scope-id (sockaddr-scope-id sa)}))\\n      (finally (ffi/free sa) (ffi/free lenp)))))\\n\\n(defn local-address\\n  \\\"{:family :ip :port :scope-id} fd is bound to, or nil when getsockname fails.\\\"\\n  [fd] (named-address c-getsockname fd))\\n\\n(defn peer-address\\n10:#   jolt repl | path | <task>     REPL, print roots, or a deps.edn task\\n44:  for candidate in \\\"$root\\\"/.cache/local/chezscheme-*/bin/scheme chez chezscheme; do\\n77:# Dev source mode opts OUT of the per-namespace AOT cache: the compiler is\\n78:# volatile here (a \\\"dev\\\" version tag wouldn't invalidate the cache across edits),\\n79:# and devboot already covers startup. A built jolt binary defaults the cache ON.\\n83:# Dev boot cache: precompiled target/dev/flat.so loads the runtime ~10x faster\\n85:# The cache is not used for REPL (interactive stdin needed) or when the first\\n86:# arg is \\\"repl\\\" (explicit REPL command).\\n88:# JOLT_NO_DEVCACHE=1 forces source mode with the cache present and fresh. The two\\n89:# are not interchangeable: the cached image bakes jolt.main's require closure and\\n92:# has to say so, or it silently tests the cached one instead.\\n96:use_cache=0\\n121:    use_cache=1\\n125:# The devcache entry is a checked-in script sharing cli.ss's tail (cli-tail.ss)\\n127:if [ \\\"$use_cache\\\" = 1 ]; then\\n128:  exec ${CHEZ} --script host/chez/cli-devcache.ss \\\"$@\\\"\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\ncodex-confirm.stdout.log:43:{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_21\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/bash -lc '. build/tools/jolt-env.sh && timeout 120 \\\"$JOLT_HOME/bin/jolt\\\" -Srepro -M:jolt -e \\\"$(cat <<'\\\"'CLJ'\\n(load-file \\\\\\\"build/j7-review/native-injection.clj\\\\\\\")\\n(fake-host\\n (fn []\\n   (let [r (traffic) seam (bind-seam r) oa ffi/alloc of ffi/free live (atom {})\\n         result\\n         (with-redefs [ffi/alloc (fn [n]\\n                                  (if (= n 4)\\n                                    (throw (ex-info \\\\\\\"injected socklen allocation failure\\\\\\\" {}))\\n                                    (let [p (oa n)] (swap\\\"'! live assoc p n) p)))\\n                       ffi/free (fn [